sections.s2_technical:
A monolith keeps all functionality in one process and usually one database. The advantages early on are significant: a single codebase is easy to understand, a single deployment is simple, calls between modules are fast in-process method calls, and a transaction can span the whole system because it is one database. For a small team or an early-stage product, this speed and simplicity are exactly what you want.

```java
// Monolith: in-process calls, one transaction spans modules
@Transactional
public void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
    accountModule.debit(from, amount);      // same process
    accountModule.credit(to, amount);        // same transaction
    auditModule.record(from, to, amount);    // all commit together
}
```

Microservices split the system by business capability, each service independently deployable with its own database, communicating over the network via REST, gRPC, or messaging. The benefits are independent scaling, so you scale only the hot service; independent deployment, so one team ships without coordinating a full-system release; fault isolation, so one service failing need not take down others; and team autonomy, where each team owns its service end to end. For a large organization at scale, these are decisive.

```java
// Microservice: network call to another service, its own database
public void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
    accountClient.debit(from, amount);       // network call to account service
    accountClient.credit(to, amount);         // no shared DB transaction
    eventBus.publish("transfer.completed", /* ... */);  // async audit
}
```

The costs of microservices are real and often underestimated: there is no distributed transaction across services by default, so you need patterns like sagas to maintain consistency; network calls can fail and add latency, requiring retries, timeouts, and circuit breakers; data is spread across services, complicating queries; and you need mature operations, with service discovery, centralized logging, tracing, and CI/CD per service. A senior nuance is that premature microservices on a small team are a classic mistake, trading simple in-process calls for a distributed system before the scale or team structure justifies it; the pragmatic path is often a well-modularized monolith first, then extracting services along clear seams when a specific scaling or deployment pain appears.

